Commit message:

core: integrity shows, counts and applies a proposal only while a fresh run still finds it (INT-275)

What was false: core integrity fix listed every pending_fixes row it had ever written, 7 8 17 19 20, without re-running a check, while the doctor counted a fresh run and said 1. A drift was keyed by its description, which carries the version, so every cargo bump added a row and applying an old one wrote an old version. The move arm ignored its row and moved any future/ intent whose text contained status: complete. Row 8 belonged to a check that no longer exists.

What is true now: a proposal has an identity, (check_name, subject), with its fix stored as data (tool and version, or from and to). pending_fixes gains subject, retired_at and retired_reason (additive; drift rows backfilled from action_data). After every run a reconcile retires a pending row as check removed, pre-275 row, superseded by #N, or resolved; retired is not applied and stays as history. fix runs the suite and lists only pending rows, with one history line. apply re-runs that row's check alone, refuses in one line with a non-zero exit when the row is no longer true, and carries out the stored fix. heal goes through the same refusing apply. One status reader, frontmatter_status.

How it was watched: four cargo tests red on the old code then green (ghost, identity, resolved, stale apply); the move class red on the deployed build in a stand-in HOME, green as a cargo test. On the real table: 8 check removed, 7 pre-275 row, 17 and 19 superseded by #20, apply on each refused with registry and ledger hashes unchanged; #20 applied, one registry line changed; doctor count equals fix. Doors: engine 52/52, nsh-test 215/215, d 0 failed. Findings F-0011 (swaylock via Niri) and F-0012 (integrity_pct is presence, not proportion) filed by this intent, not fixed.

